  While weary cities trade their grime and dust
  For tranquil pools where paper vessels glide.

  In quiet rooms the kettle starts to sing,
  A steady warmth against the chilling glass.
  Outside, the deluge softens everything,
  The weeping willow and the tangled grass.
  It drinks the moss, it bathes the furrowed bark,
  It fills the hollow nests of autumn leaves,
  And hums a velvet lullaby through dark
  Beneath the sheltering shadow of the eaves.

  Here burdens drown and restless thoughts unbind,
  Submerged within the rhythm of the squall.
  A gentle balm across a troubled mind,
  Where every drop is grace allowed to fall.
  Each silver thread ties heaven to the soil,
  A tender stitch that mends the fractured ground,
  Absolving sorrow, quieting our toil,
  With sweet, persistent symphony of sound.

  Until the storm begins to cease,
  And shadows drift into the dawn,
  The world awakens into peace,
  With sorrow gone.

